      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "mms_steady_anisotropic_neumann",
          "description": "Manufactured steady solution u*(x,y) = cos(pi x) cos(pi y) with c = 1, tensor K = [[1, 1/2], [1/2, 1]] (positive definite). u* has zero normal derivative on all four walls of the unit box, so the homogeneous Neumann walls (qXL=qXR=qYL=qYR=0) are exact and the cell-centered even-reflection ghost f[0,j]=f[1,j] reproduces cos's exact even extension about the wall - the only error is the O(dx^2+dy^2) truncation of the anisotropic operator L, whose cross-derivative half is central_Dxy_neumann_bc. Derivation: the continuous balance is u_t = c(L u* + S) = 0 with S = -L u* = 2 pi^2 cos(pi x) cos(pi y) - pi^2 sin(pi x) sin(pi y). WELL-POSEDNESS: with per-axis Neumann walls the discrete energy estimate retains a residual conormal boundary flux n.K grad u = Kxy u_tangential the imposed normal condition does not kill, so L is not guaranteed negative definite; the manufactured u* happens to make that boundary term vanish (u^2 is equal at the wall endpoints), and empirically the stiff dissipative bulk keeps the integration stable at the tested resolutions (order-2 drift). The t=0 assertion pins the ic wiring; the t=0.1 assertion bounds the truncation drift against the same steady reference. References evaluate at cell centers x_i = (i-1/2)/NX, y_j = (j-1/2)/NY, spelled inline per esm-spec 6.6.5 (build-time evaluation folds literals and metaparameters only).",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.1
          },
